{
  "dom_id": "step-q76014",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Find the value of the constant $c$ that makes the function $h(x)$ below continuous at $x=1.$\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{4}^{cx}\\,\\,, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 6x+10, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x) & =h(1) \\\\  & ={4}^{c(1)} \\\\  & ={4}^{c},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(6x+10) \\\\  & =6(1)+10 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{c} & =16 \\\\ {4}^{c} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$ for $c=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
